this morning president elect Donald  Trump officially named his pick to lead  the US Environmental Protection Agency  Oklahoma Attorney General Scott Pruett  the swearing-in ceremony took place in  the White House treaty room with Supreme  Court Justice Samuel Alito administering  the oath Trump's EPA Chief came into  office determined to end what he dubbed  the agency's anti-energy agenda by  loosening requirements on carbon  emissions and eliminating land use  restrictions Pruett has set out to  transform the agency he leads perhaps to  a greater extent than any of Trump's  other cabinet appointees we as an agency  and we as a nation could be both pro  energy and jobs and pro-environment  one of his biggest targets a  controversial rule on water pollution  put in place by the Obama administration  you had a power grab that was taking  place the EPA when they were trying to  assert jurisdiction over land use  decisions that were being made by  private property owners in cities and  towns and states across the country  progressives were outraged he is going  to dismantle everything in place the air  you breathe the water you drink to  understand why property rights advocates  applauded Pruitt's move consider the  case of fourth-generation farmer John  Duarte who's fought a protracted and  costly legal battle with federal  regulators over how to tell his 450 acre  farm in Tehama County California if the  government can do this to us there's no  family in America who can farm their  land without concern the government can  come onto their property and take  everything they own my family's farming  history goes back to my  great-grandfather's we produce  grapevines almond trees pistachio trees  walnuts avocados and a few other minor  crops the government is saying that our  tillage of this ground to plant a wheat  crop violated the Clean Water Act in  2012 the Army Corps of Engineers working  in conjunction with the EPA accused  Wardy of damaging wetland features on  his property the penalty over 30 million  in fines and restoration fees but  getting permission before plowing wasn't  really an option the average time  to obtain Clean Water Act permit is  close to two years and the average cost  just to hire the consultants and do the  studies to get permits approaches a  quarter of a million dollars attorney  Anthony Francois with the Pacific Legal  Foundation represented warty in his case  against the government clearly if you  had to undertake that kind of cost and  time just to get the necessary permit to  plow your fields every year you're not  gonna grow a lot of food  awardees trouble stemmed from a  provision in the Clean Water Act known  as the waters of the United States rule  that was meant to better protect large  bodies of water by regulating use of the  streams ponds and ditches that flow into  them but in a power grab by agency  bureaucrats Obama's EPA expanded the  power granted by this provision using it  as licensed to micromanage private land  use to the point of absurdity the agency  accused Wardy of mismanaging the wetland  areas located on his property claiming  that his 4-inch plow furrows created  small mountain ranges not those  mountains these ones that's what we have  right here these between these furrows  they're saying that the bottom of this  furrow is still a wetland but the top of  this 405 inches higher is a converted  upland attorney-general's from 31 states  including Pruitt challenged the Obama  administration's overreached on the  Clean Water Act the broad trend of  environmental regulation during the  Obama administration was to use the  coercive threat or reality of regulation  simply to try to shut down entire  industries and entire types of economic  activity that's university of virginia  law professor Jason Scott Johnston he  says that the Obama administration  expanded the definition of wetlands  beyond the parameters set by the Supreme  Court in a 2007 decision they have  promulgated the definition of wetlands  which clearly contradicts what the  Supreme Court said they're gonna go out  and regulate things that they consider  to be wetlands even though this Court  has said they're not EPA so-called  waters of the United States rule is one  of the worst examples of federal  regulation and it has truly run amok  Trump administration has started the  process of repealing the rule but faces  multiple legal hurdles and getting it  off the books could take several years  faced with millions of dollars in fines  duarte settled his case in August for  1.1 million  he hopes Pruitt's focus on regulatory  rollback will restore the property  rights of farmers we become peasants  where these federal prosecutors can come  in like the Sheriff of Nottingham decide  for themselves well they think a family  can pay decide for themselves if they  want to destroy a family and take their  land away if the federal prosecutors can  come on this farm with this set of facts  there is no farm in America that is safe  from this kind of prosecution  [Music]
 

